The test case `("_x\u6100\u6200\u6300\u6400_", "_x\u6100\u6200\u6300\u6400_")` 
shows that when we have actual Unicode characters (not UTF-16-BE encoded hex 
sequences), they should NOT be matched.

When `_x\u6100\u6200\u6300\u6400_` is encoded as UTF-16-BE:
- `_` becomes `\x00_`
- `x` becomes `\x00x`
- `\u6100` becomes `\x61\x00`
- `\u6200` becomes `\x62\x00`
- `\u6300` becomes `\x63\x00`
- `\u6400` becomes `\x64\x00`
- `_` becomes `\x00_`

So the byte sequence would be: `\x00_\x00x\x61\x00\x62\x00\x63\x00\x64\x00\x00_`

But when `_x000D_` is encoded as UTF-16-BE:
- `_` becomes `\x00_`
- `x` becomes `\x00x`
- `0` becomes `\x000`
- `0` becomes `\x000`
- `D` becomes `\x00D`
- `_` becomes `\x00_`

So the byte sequence would be: `\x00_\x00x\x000\x000\x00D\x00_`

The difference is that in the first case, we have `\x61\x00` (which is `a` in 
UTF-16-LE), but in the second case, we have `\x000` (which is `0` in UTF-16-BE).

The regex should match sequences where each hex digit is preceded by `\x00`. The
correct regex should be:
`rb"\x00_\x00x((?:\x00[0-9a-fA-F]){4})\x00_"`

Now let me implement the changes.

